Good morning, City Council (Bcc'd),

A gentle reminder, PD is looking to coordinate a briefing tomorrow and the following Tuesday; if you can kindly respond to confirm your availability this will greatly assist with scheduling.

Thank you,
Martin

From: Martin McTaggart
Sent: Thursday, August 20, 2026 4:36 PM
Cc: Ed Barberini <ebarberini@cityofsanmateo.org>; Anthony Riccardi <ARiccardi@cityofsanmateo.org>; Alex Khojikian <akhojikian@cityofsanmateo.org>
Subject: Council Poll: Axon Technology Briefing Availability
Importance: High

Good afternoon, City Council (Bcc'd),
The Police Department is exploring additional technology solutions available through Axon that could support the City's public safety operations. As part of that exploration, Axon has offered to provide Council members with an IN PERSON informational briefing and demonstration of its technology.
The briefing will focus primarily on Axon's Automated License Plate Recognition (ALPR) technology, including its Outpost fixed license plate reader and video solution. Axon will also provide an overview of other public safety technology, including Dedrone airspace security, as well as information regarding data security and privacy, ethical oversight, potential contract scope, and financial considerations.
The purpose of this briefing is informational only. It is intended to provide Council members with an opportunity to learn more about the technology and ask questions directly of Axon representatives. Participation does not represent a commitment by the City to purchase or implement any particular product.
To accommodate Council schedules and allow for a more conversational presentation and Q&A, Axon has proposed several identical one-hour small-group sessions (maximum of two Council Members per session).
Please reply to me at your earliest opportunity and indicate ALL of the time slots below that you would be available to attend. We will use the responses to coordinate the sessions.
Tuesday, August 25:

  *   1:00-2:00 p.m.
  *   2:00-3:00 p.m.
  *   3:00-4:00 p.m.
  *   4:00-5:00 p.m.
Tuesday, September 1:

  *   1:00-2:00 p.m.
  *   2:00-3:00 p.m.
  *   3:00-4:00 p.m.
  *   4:00-5:00 p.m.
Thank you, and please let me know if you have any questions.
Best wishes,
Martin

